RR1 is correct. The note does not establish its advertised closure of all subfield frame-less modules. The main gaps are the restriction to relative binary modules, the passage from generator height to useful small-value counts, and RR4’s missing order-index hypotheses.

I read the note, the earlier blind round, and src/grdeg/, and ran the ledger, relative-resultant diagnostics, and tests. All 13 tests pass. They verify specific arithmetic and geometry examples, not the general closure claims.

1. Ledger: CORRECT-WITH-FIXES.

The exponent calculation is correct under the stated size model. More precisely, it is κ_gen·x, not κ_gen itself, that has x-exponent 1+cρ−δ.

Writing E for the exponent of log X gives

    E = max(1+cρ−δ, δ).

Balancing the terms gives δ=(1+cρ)/2. With ρ=α, the smoothness requirement E≤2α becomes

    1+cα ≤ 4α,
    α ≥ 1/(4−c).

Thus the formal optimum is 1/4 at c=0 and 1/3 at c=1. The intermediate values printed by ledger.py are correct.

Two qualifications matter.

First, the time budget initially gives ρ≤α. Equality is the optimal choice in this interpolation model, rather than a consequence of the time budget alone. The complete relation-yield inequality is C≥log B+u log u, with suitable lower-order terms; suppressing log B does not change this exponent calculation.

Second, “achievable factoring exponent” is too strong. This is the optimum of a conditional ledger. It neither constructs suitable modules nor proves that their candidates have the assumed sizes or supply independent relations.

The note’s §1 disclosure about H5_r is substantially honest. Indeed, if the ternary box supplies at least B candidates, then 3^r≥B, whereas its boundary-error allowance is at least

    r(D+1)3^(r−1) ≥ r(D+1)B/3.

So H5_r fails decisively. The typical-size assumption is doing essential work.

The code has not incorporated the note’s corrections. ledger.py:109–115 still prints that exceeding “Schmidt’s generic bound” is required and that Δ_F=0 is the only escape. Both statements are wrong. Schmidt’s unrestricted bound cannot be exceeded by its linked subset; the conjectural congruence saving can fail. Also, Δ_F=0 does not mean frame-less, and failure of the lattice-counting hypotheses remains another escape. Schmidt’s actual theorem is an unrestricted height count with degree- and dimension-dependent constants: [Schmidt, 1993](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01311215).

2. Proposition RR: WRONG as an unrestricted proposition; several components survive with precise repairs.

RR1: CORRECT.

Let I=ker ψ. Since ψ is unital, its image contains every integer residue, so it is automatically surjective and

    [O_k:I] = N.

A common root v over Z/N gives a common monic factor T−v. Hence the resultant vanishes even though Z/N is not a field. Equivalently, use the resultant’s Bézout identity. Resultant specialization is valid here because the relative minimal polynomials are monic.

For R≠0,

    (R) ⊆ I ⊆ O_k,

and therefore

    |Norm(R)| = [O_k:(R)]
              = [O_k:I][I:(R)]
              = N[I:(R)].

This proves divisibility by the entire N, including prime powers. No factoring assumption or equidistribution hypothesis is involved. The same index argument works in a full-rank order containing R.

I found no counterexample to this statement under its actual hypotheses.

RR2: CORRECT-WITH-FIXES.

The heights need an explicit definition. For monic relative polynomials, a suitable choice is

    h₁ = Στ Σα:τf(α)=0 log max(1,|α|),

and similarly for h₂, counting all embeddings τ:k→C.

The elementary root-product estimate is

    log |Norm(R)|
        ≤ n′h₁ + nh₂ + enn′ log 2.

For n=n′ it yields exactly

    h₁+h₂ ≥ x/n − en log 2
           = e x/d − d log 2.

Thus the final height inequality in the note is valid with these definitions.

However, the preceding displayed estimate with “+O(d)” in log |Norm(R)| is not the general root-product estimate. Its error is O(enn′), becoming O(d) only after division by n in the equal-degree case.

This distinction is real. For odd prime n, take

    f(T)=(T−1)^n−2,
    g(T)=(T+1)^n−2.

Over Q, their root-height sums satisfy

    n(h₁+h₂) = (0.64613…+o(1))n²,

whereas

    log |Res(f,g)| = (log 2+o(1))n².

The difference is quadratic, not O(n). Passing to k=Q(i) doubles both quantities; these polynomials remain irreducible there.

Alternatively, Hadamard gives a clean estimate using coefficient 2-norm heights, but those are different heights. The distinction between coefficient norms and generator heights must be retained. Compare the precise coefficient-norm resultant inequality in [Coxon, Lemma 3.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1412.6011).

There is also a numerical typo: for the N=65 quadratic example,

    R=65²,
    Norm(R)=65⁴=17,850,625.

The note’s expression “65²·274625” equals 65⁵. The code computes the correct value.

RR3: CORRECT-WITH-FIXES for relative binary modules; WRONG as a closure of all subfield modules.

For r=2e and D=2d,

    e x/d = r x/D.

Thus the height floor has the desired r-dependence. The occurrences of “κ_gen ~ r·x/(2D)” mix a dimensionless coefficient with a logarithmic size and introduce an unnecessary factor two. The appropriate coefficient scale is e/d=r/D.

Under the additional typical-size ledger, the corrected height bound does restore α≥1/3. The −O(d) term cannot simply be dropped, but it can be retained alongside the ledger’s degree cost. If log X controls both h₁+h₂ and d, then

    x/n ≤ h₁+h₂+O(d) = O(log X).

With e comparable to r, this supplies the required exponent obstruction.

That remains a conditional size argument. H5_r controls a particular lattice-versus-volume error; it does not turn generator height into a small-value counting theorem. In particular, subfield unit actions can give infinite sublevel volume. RR does not supply the missing replacement count.

RR4: WRONG as written.

Even with perfectly aligned binary modules, “a product square on both sides is already a square in k” is false.

For example, let

    k=Q(i),  K=K′=k(s),  s²=14,
    f=g=T²−14.

The element 14 is a square in both ambient fields and is not a square in k. Also, (1+s)²=15+2s is a common square outside k.

The correct argument under good-order assumptions is different: f=g identifies the coordinate fields; aligned modules give the same algebraic element; agreeing reduction maps send its two square roots to equal values up to one global sign. That proves triviality without descent to k.

Without the order-index conditions, even “only trivial congruences” is false. Here is an exact example satisfying RR’s displayed polynomial hypotheses:

    N=65,
    k=Q(i),
    K=K′=Q(i,√14),
    s=s′=65√14,
    f=g=T²−59150.

Use two reduction maps with

    i ↦ 8 on both sides,
    √14 ↦ 53 on side 1,
    √14 ↦ 27 on side 2.

Both square-root residues square to 14 modulo 65, and both send s to zero. Thus the relative polynomials share v=0 and R=0. Nevertheless, the common candidate 14 has square-root images yielding

    gcd(53−27,65)=13.

The maps agree on O_k[s] but differ on the larger order containing √14. The relevant index is divisible by N.

This is not a factoring improvement: the two supplied maps already reveal 13, before collecting relations. It illustrates the weaker conclusion of Lemma CC—precomputable splits—and why its order-index hypotheses cannot be omitted.

Finally, f and g must be adapted to the actual paired module generators. In D1, choosing √3 as a field generator on both sides gives f=g, although the module generators differ by N. Choosing the paired generators correctly gives

    f=T²−3,
    g=(T−N)²−3,
    Res(f,g)=N²(N²−12).

An arbitrary presentation of the fields cannot support RR4.

“RR subsumes SL”: CORRECT-WITH-FIXES.

RR replaces the construction-hardness argument for the aligned, nonzero-resultant height obstruction. It is stronger and avoids the unsupported fixed-root hardness claim.

But “N^{o(1)} generators make R too small” is not sufficient when degrees grow. The actual contradiction requires

    n′h₁+nh₂+enn′ log 2 < x.

Subpolynomial individual heights can still produce a resultant norm exceeding N after multiplication over growing numbers of embeddings.

3. Scope honesty: WRONG as an exhaustive open list.

The most important omission is growing relative module rank.

Write

    r=em,

where m is the module’s rank over k. RR’s argument supplies e x/d. It supplies a penalty proportional to r x/d when m is bounded, including m=2. If e is fixed and m grows, that deduction fails.

For e=x^ε, the RR term has exponent

    1+ε−δ,

whereas the proposed closure needs 1+ρ−δ. Identifying ε with ρ silently assumes bounded relative rank.

Here is a concrete nonbinary subfield frame-less example:

    N=65,  k=Q(i),
    K=k(s),    s³=14,
    K′=k(s′),  s′³=79.

Use

    i ↦ 8,
    s,s′ ↦ 14,

since 8²≡−1 and 14³≡14≡79 modulo 65.

Take the six paired generators

    (1,i,s,is,s²,is²),
    (1,i,s′,is′,s′²,is′²).

They have common residues

    (1,8,14,47,1,8).

This has r=6 and D=12. Its twelve factor rows lie in two complementary three-dimensional blocks, according to the two embeddings of k. A seven-row frame is impossible: either it already overfills a block, or deleting a row from the other block leaves too many rows in the first block. All twelve factor directions are distinct.

For A,B,C∈k the relative norm is

    A³+14B³+14²C³−42ABC,

with 79 replacing 14 on the other side. These are distinct forms, not repeated norms.

RR remains true:

    R=−65³,  Norm(R)=65⁶.

The point is not that this example violates RR1. It shows that frame-less subfield modules need not be relative binary. For general relative rank m, a two-generator resultant does not manufacture the missing factor m.

The displayed power-basis construction pays additional height and gives no improved algorithm. Excluding all alternative growing-rank modules requires another argument, such as a relative small-value theorem. RR alone does not provide it.

The open list should therefore include:

(a) Non-subfield frame-less configurations and decoupled fibre products, as stated.

(b) Subfield configurations of growing relative rank, and the missing height-to-count passage even for relative binary configurations.

(c) Boxes outside H5_r and the exceptional families addressed conjecturally by LP_exc.

(d) Any growing-rank constant window excluded only by H7_r or other uniformity assumptions. rank3 itself lists such a window inside H5_r.

N-dependent subfields require a separate construction and size ledger, but are not intrinsically outside RR1. Neither its statement nor its proof requires k to be fixed independently of N. If the hypotheses hold for k=k_N, divisibility still follows. What must additionally be charged is the base-field presentation, basis, and associated heights.

Also, ordinary tower-NFS literature is principally about finite-field discrete logarithms. It does not itself prove an integer-factoring lower bound for every analogous tower construction; see [Sarkar–Singh’s polynomial-selection treatment](https://www.aimsciences.org/article/doi/10.3934/amc.2019028).

The available counting literature does not fill these gaps automatically. [Bean–Thunder, 1996](https://doi.org/10.1112/jlms/54.1.39) gives a volume inequality under nonzero discriminant. [Thunder, 2001](https://annals.math.princeton.edu/2001/153-3/p07) requires finite type for its uniform integral-point bound. [Stewart, 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01097) extends counting to essentially finite type, but does not directly provide the required congruence saving with controlled constants in the proposed growing-degree regime.

4. Verdict: the categorical “No” is WRONG as a theorem; “no successful construction was found” is CORRECT.

I found no sub-L[1/3] factoring algorithm and no counterexample to RR1. The corrected relative-resultant argument is a useful obstruction for aligned relative binary modules under a stated size model.

The most concrete additional crack is a fixed-degree subfield with growing relative module rank. RR then charges e rather than r. A construction would still need inexpensive linking, roughly exp(C) enumerable candidates at log X=O(x^{2α+o(1)}) for α<1/3, and useful square dependencies after removing unit or duplicate relations. The six-generator example above establishes the geometry, not those algorithmic properties.

The remaining labels should be changed accordingly:

“Proposition RR [proved]” should distinguish rigorous divisibility, the corrected height estimate, conditional ledger consequences, and the repaired coincidence statement.

“Subfield frame-less case re-closed at 1/3” and “all degenerate types except non-subfield and large boxes” should be withdrawn.

D1’s coincidence sublattice should not be called “rank-2 general position.” Exactly,

    W=W′=16(a₁²−2a₂²)²,
    F=256(a₁²−2a₂²)^4.

Its factor directions repeat. The test named rank2_gp only checks W=W′.

The experimental confinement claim should remain specific to the sampled boxes and family. It is not a theorem about all subfield modules.

The defensible conclusion is: RR1 is rigorous; the relative binary height obstruction is sound after repairs; the general G_r case remains open, including more subfield configurations than the note currently admits.